  "query_preview": "..\n\nToday: Temperatures will increase ahead of a cold front with \nhighs nearing mid to upper 80s. Afternoon and evening humidity \nwill drop below 20 percent for southern areas as winds increase to\n10 to 20 mph, gusting to 30 mph in the presence of dry fuels. A \nRed Flag Warning remains in effect until 8 PM MDT tonight, \nspecifically for Fire Zones 122 and 137.\n\nTonight into Friday: A low pressure system associated with a cold\nfront will advance through the region with increasing rain \nshowers. Widespread convective showers are possible (20%-60% \nchance across the CWA) in areas north/northwest of I-94. Shortwave\ntroughs embedded aloft, may potentially heighten PoPs. \n\nSaturday: Expect a brief pause in rain with temps remaining well \nbelow seasonal norms.\n\nSunday morning through Monday morning: Temperatures will remain \nbelow normal as an overunning event begins with Pacific Moisture. \nSW flow will track a mid-level disturbance through our area with \nhigher PoPs (60%-70%) widespread throughout the CWA. There is a \nrange of possible QPF that is varying model to model. NBM is \ntrending higher, at 0.25\" - 1\". Meanwhile, other deterministic \nmodel guidance such as the NAM, suggest more conservative\nestimates at three-tenths of an inch of rain.\n\nTuesday Moving Forward: Cooler temperatures remain in place \nthrough mid- week when a signal for a ridge to the west brings a \nchance for a chinook to warm us back up to seasonal norms sometime\naround Wednesday to Friday.\n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS:\n\nA mini deviation to NBM for MinRH was performed along the southern\nborder to match up with the BYZ office for the Red Flag Warning in\nplace this afternoon. Chances have increased with widespread\nshowers this evening into Friday, albeit a small increase.\n\nConfidence is HIGH for low RH values and windy conditions raising\nfire weather concerns on today. \n\nConfidence is LOW for timing, placement, and amount of QPF of \nspecific showers through Friday, including Sunday evening\nregarding amount of precipitation. \n\nFINDLEY",
